 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "NORTH: the rest of Jersey. Gorey and Mont Orgueil, the War Tunnels, Durrell and the cliffs of the north coast."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "ELIZABETH CASTLE. The causeway is open for walkers at low tide only. If the sea is over the sand, it is not open. Believe the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "HARBOUR. Ferries for Guernsey and Poole from the pier. The Jersey Lily and the Bailiwick Belle, weather permitting."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_helier",
   "title": "St Helier",
   "text": "The town is named for a hermit. Helier, a missionary from the Low Countries, lived on a rock in the bay in the 6th century, and was killed by pirate raiders about the year 555, so the story goes. The rock is by Elizabeth Castle and is called the Hermitage. In Jèrriais the town is St Hélyi."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_elizabeth",
   "title": "Elizabeth Castle",
   "text": "Begun in the 1590s on a tidal islet, when Spain's Armada was a recent memory. Sir Walter Raleigh, Governor of Jersey from 1600, named it Fort Isabella Bellissima for Elizabeth I. Charles II sheltered here as a boy in 1646 and again in 1649 and 1650. The Germans fortified it, too. At low tide you can walk across the sand to the castle gate."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_square",
   "title": "The Royal Square",
   "text": "On 6 January 1781 a French force under Baron de Rullecourt landed at night, captured the lieutenant governor and marched into this square. Major Francis Peirson, aged 24, led the British counter-attack and won within the hour. He was killed in the fighting. John Singleton Copley painted The Death of Major Peirson, which hangs in Tate Britain."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_george",
   "title": "King George II",
   "text": "The gilded statue in the square shows George II as a Roman emperor, in the dress of the old Caesars. It was put up in 1751, and a loyal Jersey has kept it polished ever since. The islands owe loyalty to the Crown as the successor to the old Dukes of Normandy."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_liberation",
   "title": "Liberation Square",
   "text": "Jersey was occupied by German forces from 1 July 1940 until 9 May 1945. On Liberation Day crowds filled the harbour front to welcome the British troops ashore. The bronze sculpture of 1995 shows islanders raising the Union Flag, and 9 May is a public holiday in Jersey and Guernsey every year."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_sth_market",
   "title": "The Central Market",
   "text": "A Victorian covered market, opened in 1882, with a cast-iron and glass roof, a fountain in the middle and stalls of fish, flowers, fruit and cheese. A pavement full of locals shopping for dinner is the best sign it works. The fish hall is at one end, and the potatoes in spring are at the other."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_fort_regent",
   "title": "Fort Regent",
   "text": "A granite fortress built on the hill above the town between 1806 and 1814, when the island feared invasion by Napoleon's France. It was named for the Prince Regent, later George IV. It has been a leisure centre since the 1970s, with swimming pools where the cannon used to be."
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_jerriais",
   "people": "normans",
   "title": "Jèrriais",
   "text": "The Norman-French language of Jersey, spoken here since the island belonged to the Duchy of Normandy. Normandy was lost to the English crown in 1204, but the islands stayed loyal. They still toast the king as 'our Duke'. A handful of islanders speak Jèrriais today, and schools teach it. Bouônjour means good day."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_vraic",
   "title": "Vraic",
   "text": "Vraic is the Jersey word for seaweed, which islanders once gathered off the shore in carts and spread on the fields as fertiliser, or dried and burned. A day set aside for gathering it was called vraicing. It is still used on the Jersey Royal potato fields."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_jerriais_revival",
   "title": "Keeping Jèrriais",
   "local": {
    "jer": "Jèrriais"
   },
   "text": "The Société Jersiaise was founded in 1873 to study the island's history, and it has gathered Jèrriais words and stories ever since. Frank Le Maistre's dictionary appeared in 1966. Fewer people speak the language each decade, but classes, songs and the occasional festival keep it going. Mèrcie means thank you."
  }
 ],
